Certainly! To generate complete SQL queries using Spring Boot with Oracle's Universal Connection Pool (UCP), you typically follow a series of steps that involve setting up your Spring Boot application, configuring UCP, and then using a repository or service layer to handle the SQL query generation based on submitted data.

### Step 1: Set Up Spring Boot Project

1. **Create a Spring Boot Project**: You can use Spring Initializr (https://start.spring.io/) to create a new Spring Boot project. Include dependencies for Spring Data JPA, Oracle UCP, and any other necessary dependencies.

2. **Add Dependencies**: In your `pom.xml`, add the necessary dependencies for Oracle UCP and Spring Data JPA. Here’s an example:

   ```xml
   <dependency>
       <groupId>com.oracle.database.jdbc</groupId>
       <artifactId>ojdbc8</artifactId>
       <version>19.8.0.0</version>
   </dependency>
   <dependency>
       <groupId>com.oracle.database.ucp</groupId>
       <artifactId>ucp</artifactId>
       <version>19.8.0.0</version>
   </dependency>
   <dependency>
       <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
       <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-data-jpa</artifactId>
   </dependency>
   ```

### Step 2: Configure UCP in `application.properties`

You need to configure the UCP connection pool in your `application.properties` or `application.yml` file:

```properties
spring.datasource.url=jdbc:oracle:thin:@//<HOST>:<PORT>/<SERVICE_NAME>
spring.datasource.username=<YOUR_USERNAME>
spring.datasource.password=<YOUR_PASSWORD>
spring.datasource.driver-class-name=oracle.jdbc.OracleDriver
spring.datasource.type=oracle.ucp.jdbc.PoolDataSourceImpl
spring.datasource.ucp.poolName=myPool
spring.datasource.ucp.initialPoolSize=5
spring.datasource.ucp.minPoolSize=5
spring.datasource.ucp.maxPoolSize=20
spring.datasource.ucp.connectionFactoryClassName=oracle.jdbc.pool.OracleDataSource
```

### Step 3: Create Entity Classes

Define your entity classes that map to your database tables. For example:

```java
import javax.persistence.Entity;
import javax.persistence.Id;

@Entity
public class User {
    @Id
    private Long id;
    private String name;
    private String email;

    // Getters and Setters
}
```

### Step 4: Create a Repository Interface

Create a repository interface for your entity. Spring Data JPA will provide the implementation for you.

```java
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.JpaRepository;

public interface UserRepository extends JpaRepository<User, Long> {
    // Custom query methods can be defined here
}
```

### Step 5: Create a Service Layer

Create a service class to handle business logic and generate SQL queries based on submitted data.

```java
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

import java.util.List;

@Service
public class UserService {

    @Autowired
    private UserRepository userRepository;

    public User createUser(User user) {
        return userRepository.save(user);
    }

    public List<User> getAllUsers() {
        return userRepository.findAll();
    }

    // Example of generating a custom query
    public List<User> findUsersByName(String name) {
        return userRepository.findByName(name);
    }
}
```

### Step 6: Create a Controller

Create a REST controller to handle incoming requests and submit data.

```java
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;

import java.util.List;

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/users")
public class UserController {

    @Autowired
    private UserService userService;

    @PostMapping
    public User createUser(@RequestBody User user) {
        return userService.createUser(user);
    }

    @GetMapping
    public List<User> getAllUsers() {
        return userService.getAllUsers();
    }

    @GetMapping("/search")
    public List<User> findUsersByName(@RequestParam String name) {
        return userService.findUsersByName(name);
    }
}
```

### Step 7: Run Your Application

Run your Spring Boot application. You can now send HTTP requests to your endpoints to create users and retrieve them from the database.

### Conclusion

This setup allows you to generate SQL queries based on the data submitted through your REST API. Spring Data JPA abstracts much of the SQL generation, allowing you to focus on your business logic rather than the underlying SQL syntax. You can also define custom queries using JPQL or native SQL if needed.

