To extract the values of 'guid' and 'title' from a JSON object containing a node with children, you can use a programming language like Python, JavaScript, or any other language that can parse JSON. Below are examples in both Python and JavaScript.

### Example JSON Structure
Assuming you have a JSON structure like this:

```json
{
  "node": {
    "children": [
      {
        "guid": "12345",
        "title": "First Child"
      },
      {
        "guid": "67890",
        "title": "Second Child"
      }
    ]
  }
}
```

### Python Example
```python
import json

# Sample JSON data
data = '''
{
  "node": {
    "children": [
      {
        "guid": "12345",
        "title": "First Child"
      },
      {
        "guid": "67890",
        "title": "Second Child"
      }
    ]
  }
}
'''

# Parse the JSON data
json_data = json.loads(data)

# Extract 'guid' and 'title' from each child
for child in json_data['node']['children']:
    guid = child['guid']
    title = child['title']
    print(f'GUID: {guid}, Title: {title}')
```

### JavaScript Example
```javascript
// Sample JSON data
const data = {
  "node": {
    "children": [
      {
        "guid": "12345",
        "title": "First Child"
      },
      {
        "guid": "67890",
        "title": "Second Child"
      }
    ]
  }
};

// Extract 'guid' and 'title' from each child
data.node.children.forEach(child => {
  const guid = child.guid;
  const title = child.title;
  console.log(`GUID: ${guid}, Title: ${title}`);
});
```

### Output
Both examples will output:
```
GUID: 12345, Title: First Child
GUID: 67890, Title: Second Child
```

You can adapt the code to fit the specific structure of your JSON object and the programming language you are using.

