To create a JWT token for user verification in a Java server-side application with a jQuery client-side interface, you can follow these steps:

### Step 1: Generate a Secret Key

You need to generate a secret key for signing and verifying tokens. This key should be kept secure and not exposed in your client-side code.

```java
import io.jsonwebtoken.SignatureAlgorithm;
import io.jsonwebtoken.security.Keys;

import javax.crypto.SecretKey;

public class JwtUtil {
    private static final SecretKey SECRET_KEY = Keys.secretKeyFor(SignatureAlgorithm.HS256);

    public static SecretKey getSecretKey() {
        return SECRET_KEY;
    }
}
```

### Step 2: Create a JWT Token

Using the JJWT library, you can create a JWT token that includes user information.

```java
import io.jsonwebtoken.Jwts;
import io.jsonwebtoken.SignatureAlgorithm;

import java.util.Date;

public class TokenService {
    private static final long EXPIRATION_TIME = 864_000_000; // 10 days

    public String generateToken(String userId, String email) {
        return Jwts.builder()
                .setSubject(userId)
                .claim("email", email)
                .setIssuedAt(new Date())
                .setExpiration(new Date(System.currentTimeMillis() + EXPIRATION_TIME))
                .signWith(JwtUtil.getSecretKey())
                .compact();
    }
}
```

### Step 3: Client-Side jQuery Code

On the client-side, you can use jQuery to send an HTTP request with the user's credentials.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Login</title>
    <script src="https://code.jquery.com/jquery-3.6.0.min.js"></script>
</head>
<body>
    <form id="loginForm">
        <input type="text" id="username" placeholder="Username" required>
        <input type="password" id="password" placeholder="Password" required>
        <button type="submit">Login</button>
    </form>

    <script>
        $(document).ready(function() {
            $('#loginForm').on('submit', function(event) {
                event.preventDefault();
                const username = $('#username').val();
                const password = $('#password').val();

                $.ajax({
                    url: '/api/login',
                    type: 'POST',
                    contentType: 'application/json',
                    data: JSON.stringify({ username, password }),
                    success: function(response) {
                        // Store the JWT token in local storage or a cookie
                        localStorage.setItem('token', response.token);
                        alert('Login successful!');
                    },
                    error: function(xhr) {
                        alert('Login failed: ' + xhr.responseText);
                    }
                });
            });
        });
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

### Step 4: Server-Side User Verification

On the server-side, you need to verify the user's credentials and generate a JWT token if they are valid.

```java
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api")
public class AuthController {

    @PostMapping("/login")
    public ResponseEntity<?> login(@RequestBody LoginRequest loginRequest) {
        // Replace with your user verification logic
        if (isValidUser(loginRequest.getUsername(), loginRequest.getPassword())) {
            String token = new TokenService().generateToken(loginRequest.getUsername(), "user@example.com");
            return ResponseEntity.ok(new AuthResponse(token));
        } else {
            return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.UNAUTHORIZED).body("Invalid credentials");
        }
    }

    private boolean isValidUser(String username, String password) {
        // Implement your user validation logic here (e.g., check against a database)
        return "testUser".equals(username) && "testPassword".equals(password);
    }
}

class LoginRequest {
    private String username;
    private String password;

    // Getters and setters
}

class AuthResponse {
    private String token;

    public AuthResponse(String token) {
        this.token = token;
    }

    // Getter
}
```

### Step 5: Return the JWT Token

The server responds with the JWT token if the credentials are valid. The client can then store this token for future authenticated requests.

By following these steps, you can implement a basic JWT authentication system in your Java server-side application with a jQuery client-side interface.

